    "narrative": "The evidence did not settle whether staffing level explains on-time rate or whether order volume is the stronger driver of service quality. The overall result was inconclusive, with moderate confidence.\n\nBoth tested claims were weakened rather than supported. The staffing claim had some evidence behind it, but not enough to stand cleanly. The order-volume claim also had some evidence, but it did not clearly beat staffing level.\n\nThe run used a correlation analysis, then a simple regression, then another correlation analysis. It stopped because there was insufficient evidence, and three questions remained open: which factor explains more variation in on-time rate, whether the relationship is stable across the full period or driven by a few outliers, and which of the two competing claims holds, if either.",
    "statement": "Mixed evidence for: On-time rate is associated with staffing level; if staffing level changes, on-time rate changes in the same direction more often than expected by chance.; Order volume is associated with on-time rate and is a stronger driver of service quality than staffing level.",
